tyle: western] Inbar M [name-style: western] Rosso R [name-style: western] Grischke E [name-style: western] Santoro A [name-style: western] Catane R [name-style: western] Kieback DG [name-style: western] Tomczak P [name-style: western] Ackland SP [name-style: western] Orlandi F [name-style: western] Mellars L [name-style: western] Alland L [name-style: western] Tendler C [name-style: western] Group CBCS\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDawn of Advanced Molecular Medicine: Nanotechnological Advancements in Cancer Imaging and Therapy (https://openalex.org/W1989386054)\nciteturn105search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Dawn of Advanced Molecular Medicine: Nanotechnological Advancements in Cancer Imaging and Therapy ... DOI: 10.1615/critrevoncog.2014011601\n# Dawn of Advanced Molecular Medicine: Nanotechnological Advancements in Cancer Imaging and Therapy\n\nOpenAlex ID: W1989386054\n\nDOI: 10.1615/critrevoncog.2014011601\n\nPMID: 25271430\n\nType: review\n\nYear: 2014\n\nPublication date: 2014-01-01\n\n\n## Source\n\nName: Critical Reviews™ in Oncogenesis\n\nISSN-L: 0893-9675\n\nISSN: 0893-9675, 2162-6448\n\nPublisher: Begell House\n\n## Citation\n\nVolume: 19\n\nIssue: 3-4\n\nPages: 143–176\n\n## Article links\n\n  * Article website\n  * Location website\n  * Location website\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Charalambos Kaittanis (ORCID) — Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center (ROR)\n  * Travis M. Shaffer (ORCID) — Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center (ROR)\n  * Daniel L.J. Thorek (ORCID) — Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center (ROR)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructural dynamics of single molecules studied with high-speed atomic force microscopy. | GreenMedInfo (https://uat.greenmedinfo.com/abstracts/structural-dynamics-of-single-molecules-studied-with-high-speed-atomic-25549544)\nciteturn105search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; PubMed ID: ...     10.1517/17460441.2015.998195\n\nResearch Abstract\n\n# Structural dynamics of single molecules studied with high-speed atomic force microscopy.\n\nHenderson RM\n\nExpert opinion on drug discovery\n\nMar 1, 2015\n\nSources\n\n2 min read\n\n5 views\n\nShare:\n\n## Abstract\n\nINTRODUCTION: Atomic force microscopy (AFM) is a scanning probe technique that has been in use in biology to generate sub-nanometre resolution images in near-physiological environments for over 20 years. Most AFM work uses instruments that take several minutes to generate each image but instruments that can produce real-time images have recently become available and there is now a reasonable body of work published on this technique. The importance of this high-speed AFM is that dynamic events of individual macromolecules can be studied. AREAS COVERED: This review focuses on specific examples that demonstrate the potential of the technique. It covers four areas in which high-speed AFM has been used to elucidate mechanisms that are either unstudied or not clearly understood. These areas are: protein-protein interactions; DNA-protein interactions; quantification of biological processes; the use of DNA origami scaffolds as nanostructures to build and study dynamic molecular events. EXPERT OPINION: High-speed AFM shares advantages and disadvantages with conventional AFM, but it compares well in quality of data generated and in ease of use with other currently available techniques of high-resolution biological imaging. As the instruments become more widespread, the value of high-speed AFM and its potential to complement other techniques in molecular and cell biology should become more appreciated.\n\n## Affiliation\n\nUniversity of Cambridge, Department of Pharmacology , Tennis Court Road, Cambridge CB2 1PD , UK +44 1223 334053 ; [email protected].\n\n## External References\n\nPubMed ID:\n    25549544\n\nDOI:\n    10.1517/17460441.2015.998195\n\nSource:\n    https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25549544/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) 3D superresolution microscopy by supercritical angle detection (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/268236818_3D_superresolution_microscopy_by_supercritical_angle_detection)\nciteturn105search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 23 | DOI:10.1364/OE.22.029081 | OPTICS EXPRESS 29082\n#223804 - $15.00 USD\n\nReceived 25 Sep 2014; revised 3 Nov 2014; accepted 3 Nov 2014; published 13 Nov 2014\n\n(C) 2014 OSA\n\n17 November 2014 | Vol. 22, No. 23 | DOI:10.1364/OE.22.029081 | OPTICS EXPRESS 29082\n\nHere we present a fundamentally different wa y of achieving 3D resolution in localization\n\nmicroscopy without significant loss in lateral resolution, called supercritical angle localization\n\nmicroscopy (SALM), which is based on the pr inciple of surface-gen erated fluorescence.\n\nSurface-generated fluorescence occurs when a fl uorophore is in the vicinity of an interface\n\nwith different refractive indices, such as the water – coverslip interface. This fluorescence is\n\nemitted into large angles above the critical a ngle and is therefore also called supercritical\n\nangle fluorescence [10] (Fig. 1(a)). Its intensity depends approximately exponentially on the\n\ndistance of the fluorophore from the interface (Fig. 1(b)). Supe rcritical angle and far-field\n\nundercritical angle fluorescence are radially separated in the back focal plane (conjugate\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDawn of advanced molecular medicine: Nanotechnological advancements in cancer imaging and therapy - Johns Hopkins University (https://pure.johnshopkins.edu/en/publications/dawn-of-advanced-molecular-medicine-nanotechnological-advancement-4)\nciteturn105search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago;   * https://doi.org/10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601 ... Critical reviews in oncogenesis, 19(3-4), 143-176. https://doi.org/10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\nBesides using particles solely for imaging purposes, they can also carry as a payload a therapeutic agent. If both are combined within the same particle, a theranostic agent is created. The sophistication of highly developed nanotechnology targeting approaches provides a promising means for many clinical implementations and can provide improved applications for otherwise suboptimal formulations. In this review we will explore nanotechnology both for imaging and therapy to provide a general overview of the field and its impact on cancer imaging and therapy.\n\nOriginal language  | English (US)\nPages (from-to)  | 143-176\nNumber of pages  | 34\nJournal  | Critical reviews in oncogenesis\nVolume  | 19\nIssue number  | 3-4\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\nState  | Published - 2014\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Chemotherapy\n  * Drug delivery\n  * Nanoparticles\n  * Oncology\n  * Targeted therapeutics\n\n\n## Cite this\n\n  * APA\n  * Standard\n  * Harvard\n  * Vancouver\n  * Author\n  * BIBTEX\n2014;19(3-4):143-176. doi: 10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\n\nKaittanis, Charalambos ; Shaffer, Travis M. ; Thorek, Daniel L.J. et al. / Dawn of advanced molecular medicine : Nanotechnological advancements in cancer imaging and therapy. In: Critical reviews in oncogenesis. 2014 ; Vol. 19, No. 3-4. pp. 143-176.\n\n@article{babb0d32eba74f1a8c1349f106e22e07,\n\ntitle = \"Dawn of advanced molecular medicine: Nanotechnological advancements in cancer imaging and therapy\",\n\nabstract = \"Nanotechnology plays an increasingly important role not only in our everyday life (with all its benefits and dangers) but also in medicine. Nanoparticles are to date the most intriguing option to deliver high concentrations of agents specifically and directly to cancer cells; therefore, a wide variety of these nanomaterials has been developed and explored. These span the range from simple nanoagents to sophisticated smart devices for drug delivery or imaging. Nanomaterials usually provide a large surface area, allowing for decoration with a large amount of moieties on the surface for either additional functionalities or targeting. Besides using particles solely for imaging purposes, they can also carry as a payload a therapeutic agent. If both are combined within the same particle, a theranostic agent is created. The sophistication of highly developed nanotechnology targeting approaches provides a promising means for many clinical implementations and can provide improved applications for otherwise suboptimal formulations. In this review we will explore nanotechnology both for imaging and therapy to provide a general overview of the field and its impact on cancer imaging and therapy.\",\n\nkeywords = \"Chemotherapy, Drug delivery, Nanoparticles, Oncology, Targeted therapeutics\",\n\nauthor = \"Charalambos Kaittanis and Shaffer, \\{Travis M.\\} and Thorek, \\{Daniel L.J.\\} and Jan Grimm\",\n\nyear = \"2014\",\n\ndoi = \"10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\",\n\nlanguage = \"English (US)\",\n\nvolume = \"19\",\n\npages = \"143--176\",\n\njournal = \"Critical reviews in oncogenesis\",\n\nissn = \"0893-9675\",\n\npublisher = \"Begell House Inc.\",\n\nnumber = \"3-4\",\n\n}\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Dawn of advanced molecular medicine\nIn this review we will explore nanotechnology both for imaging and therapy to provide a general overview of the field and its impact on cancer imaging and therapy.\n\nAB - Nanotechnology plays an increasingly important role not only in our everyday life (with all its benefits and dangers) but also in medicine. Nanoparticles are to date the most intriguing option to deliver high concentrations of agents specifically and directly to cancer cells; therefore, a wide variety of these nanomaterials has been developed and explored. These span the range from simple nanoagents to sophisticated smart devices for drug delivery or imaging. Nanomaterials usually provide a large surface area, allowing for decoration with a large amount of moieties on the surface for either additional functionalities or targeting. Besides using particles solely for imaging purposes, they can also carry as a payload a therapeutic agent. If both are combined within the same particle, a theranostic agent is created. The sophistication of highly developed nanotechnology targeting approaches provides a promising means for many clinical implementations and can provide improved applications for otherwise suboptimal formulations. In this review we will explore nanotechnology both for imaging and therapy to provide a general overview of the field and its impact on cancer imaging and therapy.\n\nKW - Chemotherapy\n\nKW - Drug delivery\n\nKW - Nanoparticles\n\nKW - Oncology\n\nKW - Targeted therapeutics\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84906083052\n\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84906083052#tab=citedBy\n\nU2 - 10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\n\nDO - 10.1615/CritRevOncog.2014011601\n\nM3 - Article\n\nC2 - 25271430\n\nAN - SCOPUS:84906083052\n\nSN - 0893-9675\n\nVL - 19\n\nSP - 143\n\nEP - 176\n\nJO - Critical reviews in oncogenesis\n\nJF - Critical reviews in oncogenesis\n\nIS - 3-4\n\nER -\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCase Report (https://jcmcrimages.org/articles/JCMCRI-1008.pdf)\nciteturn105search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; vesting in comprehensive safety research, and streamlining regula­ ... molecular medicine: nanotechnological advancements in can­ ... doi: 10.1615/critrevoncog.2014011601.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBJRD15207 1..12 (https://ris.utwente.nl/ws/files/105862172/bjr.20150207.pdf)\nciteturn105search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.4 years ago; Dawn of advanced molecular medicine: ... 19: 143–76. doi: 10.1615/\nCritRevOncog.2014011601\n------------------------------